\begin{abstract}
Fine-tuned Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models achieve high task success yet remain collision-prone. We conduct a factorial study of twelve conditions varying five factors (gradient flow, label content, normalization type, spatial reference frame, temporal extent) to isolate how auxiliary spatial supervision affects collision avoidance during VLA fine-tuning. A $2{\times}2$ factorial crossing gradient flow with normalization reveals \textbf{two large additive main effects}: (1)~gradient flow under BatchNorm reduces Collision Avoidance Rate by 4.2\,pp (Cohen's $d{=}0.93$, large), making BN + gradient flow the sole configuration below the no-auxiliary Vanilla baseline; (2)~LayerNorm provides a consistent 3.5--4.8\,pp safety advantage over BN ($d{\approx}1.05$--$1.47$), regardless of gradient flow. The ANOVA interaction is negligible ($\eta^2{<}0.01$). The BN + gradient flow cell is both worst-performing and most unstable ($\sigma{=}0.064$, ${\geq}\,2{\times}$ all others). Spatial label content showed no detectable effect; the gradient pathway, not spatial information, determines the outcome. Guidelines: detach auxiliary gradients under BN; gradient flow is safe under LN; avoid combining BN with gradient flow.
\end{abstract}

