\subsection{Normalization and Gradient Flow Effects}
\label{sec:mechanism}

The four factorial cells form a $2{\times}2$ design crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) (Table~\ref{tab:factorial}, Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}).

\textbf{Gradient flow is harmful under BatchNorm but benign under LayerNorm.} The BN row provides the clearest signal: gradient flow reduces CAR by 4.2\,pp (Full OCC $0.725 \pm 0.064$ vs.\ Detach OCC $0.767 \pm 0.024$; Cohen's $d{=}-0.93$, large). Full OCC (BN) is the sole factorial cell falling below Vanilla ($\Delta{=}-2.0$\,pp), whereas Detach OCC recovers above it ($\Delta{=}+2.2$\,pp, $d{=}+0.88$, large). In the LN row, Full OCC+LN already exceeds Vanilla ($0.773$, $\Delta{=}+2.8$\,pp, $d{=}+1.07$, large), and Detach+LN yields the highest cell mean ($0.802 \pm 0.024$, $n{=}3$, $\Delta{=}+5.7$\,pp, $d{=}+2.36$, very large). Two main effects drive the factorial pattern ($2{\times}2$ ANOVA: gradient flow $F(1,10){=}3.20$, $p{=}0.104$, $\eta^2{=}0.18$; normalization $F(1,10){=}4.49$, $p{=}0.060$, $\eta^2{=}0.25$; interaction $F{=}0.10$, $p{=}0.76$; Appendix~\ref{app:stat_power}). First, gradient flow under BN reduces CAR by 4.2\,pp ($d{=}0.93$, large), making BN + gradient flow the sole configuration below the Vanilla baseline. Second, LN provides a consistent safety advantage over BN: $+$4.8\,pp under gradient flow ($d{=}1.05$, large) and $+$3.5\,pp without ($d{=}1.47$, very large). This is a practical threshold crossing (only the BN + gradient flow combination harms safety) rather than a statistical interaction (the factorial interaction is negligible, $\eta^2{=}0.005$).

Pairwise Welch $t$-tests with Bonferroni correction yield no significant comparisons at $\alpha{=}0.05$, consistent with small per-cell $n$ ($n{=}3$--$4$); we rely on effect sizes rather than $p$-values for inference (Appendix~\ref{app:stat_power}). The normalization main effect approaches significance ($p{=}0.060$). Both main effects amplify by ${\approx}3{\times}$ on the three physically constrained tasks (T3, T5, T6; Appendix~\ref{app:per_task_factorial}).

